        q: String.raw`<p>Two machines are compared on three SPEC CPU 2017 benchmarks. Which machine wins by the SPEC geometric mean, by the arithmetic mean of the SPEC ratios, and by total time, and what does the arithmetic mean of normalized times say when each machine is used as the reference?</p><p>Given:</p><ul><li>Reference times: 505.mcf_r 1616 s, 525.x264_r 1751 s, 548.exchange2_r 2620 s</li><li>Machine X times: mcf 240 s, x264 110 s, exchange2 170 s</li><li>Machine Y times: mcf 150 s, x264 140 s, exchange2 215 s</li><li>Each time is a single-copy run, and run-to-run noise can be ignored</li><li>"Normalized to machine \(M\)" means, for each benchmark \(i\), the ratio \(T_{M,i} / T_{\text{other},i}\) (the other machine\'s speedup over \(M\)), and the arithmetic mean of normalized times averages these ratios over the three benchmarks</li></ul>`,
        a: String.raw`<ol class="steps"><li><strong>SPEC ratios.</strong> Each ratio is the reference time over the measured time. X gets \(1616 / 240 \approx 6.733\), \(1751 / 110 \approx 15.918\), and \(2620 / 170 \approx 15.412\). Y gets \(1616 / 150 \approx 10.773\), \(1751 / 140 \approx 12.507\), and \(2620 / 215 \approx 12.186\).</li><li><strong>Geometric means.</strong><div class="math chain"><span>\(\displaystyle G_X = \left(\frac{1616\ \text{s}}{240\ \text{s}} \times \frac{1751\ \text{s}}{110\ \text{s}} \times \frac{2620\ \text{s}}{170\ \text{s}}\right)^{1/3} \)</span><span>\(\displaystyle \approx \mathbf{11.821} \)</span></div><div class="math chain"><span>\(\displaystyle G_Y = \left(\frac{1616\ \text{s}}{150\ \text{s}} \times \frac{1751\ \text{s}}{140\ \text{s}} \times \frac{2620\ \text{s}}{215\ \text{s}}\right)^{1/3} \)</span><span>\(\displaystyle \approx \mathbf{11.798} \)</span></div>X wins by the SPEC score, by a factor of about 1.002, a margin far smaller than real run noise.</li><li><strong>Arithmetic means of the ratios.</strong> X gets \((6.733 + 15.918 + 15.412) / 3 \approx 12.688\) and Y gets \((10.773 + 12.507 + 12.186) / 3 \approx 11.822\). X appears to win by 7.3%, because its two big ratios count more than its weak mcf ratio costs.</li><li><strong>Total time.</strong><div class="math chain"><span>\(\displaystyle T_X = 240 + 110 + 170\ \text{s} \)</span><span>\(\displaystyle = 520\ \text{s} \)</span></div><div class="math chain"><span>\(\displaystyle T_Y = 150 + 140 + 215\ \text{s} \)</span><span>\(\displaystyle = 505\ \text{s} \)</span></div>Y finishes the three benchmarks back to back about 3.0% sooner (\(520 / 505 \approx 1.030\)), because mcf is the longest run and Y is much faster on it.</li><li><strong>Arithmetic means of normalized times.</strong> Normalized to X, Y's speedups are \(240 / 150 = 1.6\), \(110 / 140 \approx 0.786\), and \(170 / 215 \approx 0.791\), with mean \(\approx 1.059\), so Y looks 5.9% faster. Normalized to Y, X's speedups are \(0.625\), \(\approx 1.273\), and \(\approx 1.265\), with mean \(\approx 1.054\), so X looks 5.4% faster. Each machine beats the other, which shows the arithmetic mean of ratios cannot be trusted.</li><li><strong>The reference-free check.</strong> The geometric mean of X's speedups over Y is \((0.625 \times 1.2727 \times 1.2647)^{1/3} \approx 1.002\), the same as \(G_X / G_Y\), which is the cancellation of the reference made concrete.</li><li><strong>How to report it.</strong> The machines are tied by the geometric mean (0.2% is inside any real noise), Y is 3% faster on this exact workload run once each, and X is faster on two benchmarks out of three. The honest summary gives the per-benchmark speedups (X loses mcf by a factor of 1.6 and wins the others by about 1.27), since one number hides that the machines have different strengths, likely memory latency for Y and core throughput for X.</li></ol>`
