        q: String.raw`<p>Two performance models are used to decide whether to ship a new L2 prefetcher. Measured on silicon with the prefetcher switched off and on, which model has the better absolute accuracy, which predicts the prefetcher's speedups better, and which one leads to the right decision?</p><p>Given:</p><ul><li>IPC with the prefetcher off and on (hardware, model A, model B):<ul><li>505.mcf_r: off 0.80, 0.83, 0.95, on 0.92, 0.89, 1.10</li><li>519.lbm_r: off 0.95, 0.97, 1.12, on 1.18, 1.06, 1.38</li><li>523.xalancbmk_r: off 1.30, 1.33, 1.52, on 1.37, 1.37, 1.61</li><li>525.x264_r: off 3.00, 3.05, 3.50, on 3.01, 3.10, 3.51</li><li>531.deepsjeng_r: off 1.90, 1.92, 2.25, on 1.88, 1.98, 2.22</li></ul></li><li>Absolute accuracy: MAPE of model IPC against hardware IPC over all 10 measurements, with errors relative to hardware</li><li>Speedup of a benchmark: IPC on over IPC off (each benchmark runs the same instructions both ways)</li><li>Decision rule: ship if the geometric-mean speedup over the five benchmarks is at least 1.05</li><li>Compare speedup rankings with Spearman's rank correlation (no ties occur)</li></ul>`,
        a: String.raw`<ol class="steps"><li><strong>Absolute accuracy.</strong> Model A's IPCs are mostly within a few percent of the hardware's (its largest miss is lbm with the prefetcher on, \(1.06 / 1.18 - 1 \approx -10.2\%\)), with a MAPE of about <strong>3.3%</strong>. Model B is high on every measurement, by 16.6% to 19.6% (for example \(0.95 / 0.80 - 1 = 18.75\%\) on mcf off), with a MAPE of about <strong>17.7%</strong>, all of it bias. By absolute accuracy, A is far better.</li><li><strong>Hardware speedups.</strong> mcf \(0.92 / 0.80 = 1.150\), lbm \(1.18 / 0.95 \approx 1.242\), xalancbmk \(1.37 / 1.30 \approx 1.054\), x264 \(3.01 / 3.00 \approx 1.003\), deepsjeng \(1.88 / 1.90 \approx 0.989\). The prefetcher helps the streaming and pointer-heavy memory-bound benchmarks and slightly hurts deepsjeng, which it pollutes.</li><li><strong>Model A's speedups.</strong> About 1.072, 1.093, 1.030, 1.016, and 1.031. It underestimates mcf by 7.8 percentage points and lbm by 14.9, and it predicts a 3.1% gain on deepsjeng, where the hardware loses 1.1%. Its mean absolute speedup error is about 6.1 points.</li><li><strong>Model B's speedups.</strong> About 1.158, 1.232, 1.059, 1.003, and 0.987, each within 1 percentage point of the hardware, with a mean absolute speedup error of about 0.53 points. Its 16.6% to 19.6% optimism is nearly the same with the prefetcher on and off, so it cancels in the ratio, as \(1 + e_B - e_A\) predicts.</li><li><strong>Rank correlation.</strong> The hardware ranks the speedups lbm, mcf, xalancbmk, x264, deepsjeng (ranks 2, 1, 3, 4, 5 in the order given). Model B gives the same ranks, so \(\rho_s = 1\). Model A ranks xalancbmk 4th, x264 5th, and deepsjeng 3rd, so the rank differences are 0, 0, \(-1\), \(-1\), and 2, and \(\sum d^2 = 6\).<div class="math chain"><span>\(\displaystyle \rho_s = 1 - \frac{6 \times 6}{5 \times (5^2 - 1)} \)</span><span>\(\displaystyle = \mathbf{0.7} \)</span></div></li><li><strong>Geometric-mean speedups and the decision.</strong> The hardware's is \((1.150 \times 1.2421 \times 1.0538 \times 1.0033 \times 0.9895)^{1/5} \approx 1.0837\). Model A gives about 1.0482, below 1.05, so <strong>A says don't ship</strong>. Model B gives about 1.0838, so <strong>B says ship</strong>, and B is right.</li><li><strong>Why.</strong> A's small absolute error comes from errors that differ between the two configurations: it is accurate with the prefetcher off and misses much of its benefit, which suggests it models the prefetcher's timeliness or the DRAM bandwidth it uses poorly. B's large error is shared (an idealized front end or a too-short pipeline, for example) and the prefetcher does not interact with it. For a prefetcher decision, validate the model on prefetcher deltas. The risk with B is that its shared optimism could stop cancelling for a change that touches what it idealizes, so its relative accuracy has to be checked for each kind of change, not assumed.</li></ol>`
